  "generated_text": "1. First, note that each friend must have at least one pencil. Therefore, we can start by giving each friend one pencil. This leaves us with 3 pencils to distribute freely among the three friends.\n\n2. Now, we need to find the number of ways to distribute 3 identical pencils among 3 friends. This is a classic \"stars and bars\" problem in combinatorics.\n\n3. The \"stars and bars\" theorem states that the number of ways to distribute \\( n \\) identical items into \\( k \\) distinct groups is given by the binomial coefficient \\( \\binom{n+k-1}{k-1} \\).\n\n4. In our case, \\( n = 3 \\) and \\( k = 3 \\). So, we need to calculate \\( \\binom{3+3-1}{3-1} = \\binom{5}{2} \\).\n\n5. \\( \\binom{5}{2} = \\frac{5!}{2!(5-2)!} = \\frac{5 \\times 4}{2 \\times 1} = 10 \\).\n\nTherefore, the number of ways the three friends can distribute the pencils is \\(\\boxed{10}\\).",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[10, '10']",
    "gold_answer": "The largest number of pencils that any friend can have is four. There are 3 ways that this can happen: $(4,1,1)$, $(1,4,1)$ and $(1,1,4)$. There are 6 ways one person can have 3 pencils: $(3,2,1)$, $(3,1,2)$, $(2,3,1)$, $(2,1,3)$, $(1,2,3)$ and $(1,3,2)$. There is only one way all three can have two pencils each: $(2,2,2)$. The total number of possibilities is $3+6+1=\\boxed{10}$.",
    "is_correct": true
  },
